|~ `::bind`, the step that declares a name, and what is declared under
   it: its constructor and the refusals of a declaration.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence], [QSNAM#declare]. ~|

:bind
  |~~ A declaration, `:name body;`, stands at the head of its text, before
      the query, and adds a word with its page to the scope of the text,
      its `::binding`: a kind, a verb under the label of the values it
      serves, `:number/double mul 2; 5 | double` answering `10`, a page
      or a law under a noun, a node of data under an address, and a value
      under a bare name, which the query reads where a value stands,
      `:rate 2; [1 2] | map (mul rate)` answering `[2 4]`. A body whose
      head is a verb, or a group whose head is one, declares a program,
      which runs on the subject wherever its name stands,
      `:adult (get /age | gte 18); [{:age 20}] | filter adult`. A program
      carries the values of its text it reads, written at its head where
      it is declared, `:limit 4; :big (gt limit); [1 5 10] | filter big`
      answering `[5 10]`. Any other body reads no subject and is computed
      once: a literal, a quote, a name, a tag name, a doc, a group, or a
      chain, a tag name and the commands joined to it up to the `;`, which
      builds a word. A quote stays as it is written, its values given by
      `fill`. A value of the subject is named for the steps after it by
      `with`, `{:price 4 :qty 3} | with [/price /qty] (price | mul qty)`
      answering `12`. The declarations of a text share its scope. A group
      and a quote a verb runs each close the names declared inside, the
      groups of a template `get` runs are forks of their own, and a name is
      declared once in its scope. A failure of a declaration is the
      failure of its text, which declares nothing and runs no query, and
      a text of declarations alone answers the set of the addresses it
      declared, `:Box |~~ A box. ~~|` answering `#[::Box]`.

      In a quote the step is a record, `~(:rate 2;) | first` answering
      `::bind{:name :rate :body 2}`: `:name` the keyword it binds,
      `:docs` its pages and `:body` its body, the step after its name and
      its pages, which runs once where the declaration stands.
      ::link~(::binding) ::link~(::qlang/names)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts
        |~~ The record of a declaration, `:name`, `:docs` and `:body`,
            built into its step.
        ~~| ::map
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::bind` builds the step of a declaration from its record, a
            map; a payload of another kind is refused, a map under another
            tag among them.
            ::link~(::bind)
        ~~|
    | throws :PayloadNotSchemaError
        |~~ A `::bind` is a map of `:name`, a keyword, `:docs`, a vector
            of a doc at least, and `:body`, a step, and `:field` names the
            field that does not fit: a tag is no name a declaration binds,
            a declaration binds a body or a page, and a doc is no body,
            which reads as the page, a doc bound as the value the body of a
            group; the body is the step a quote holds, so the declaration
            `:inc add 1;` is
            `get {:name :inc :body (~(add 1) | first)} | tag ::bind`.
            ::link~(::bind)
        ~~|
        ::call/PayloadNotSchemaError
    | throws :NameNotCallableError
        |~~ A declaration names what a step calls, names joined by `/`,
            each a letter or `_` and the letters, digits, `_` and `-` after
            it, and no word of a literal, which a step reads as the value,
            but for a label: `:"a b"` names nothing a step calls. `:name`
            holds the name, and `:message` what the rule says of it.
            ::link~(::bind) ::link~(::qlang/names)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :message |~~ What the rule of a name says of the name. ~~| ::string
          | wires :name |~~ The name asked for, a keyword or a tag. ~~| #[::keyword ::tag])
    | throws :LiteralComputesError
        |~~ A body that is a literal computes nothing: a name or a group
            inside it is refused, and `:message` writes the template `get`
            builds the shape from where no part of it reads the subject,
            `get [(5 | add 1)]`, and names the page of `get` where a part
            does, `[first last]` among them, which a program builds on the
            subject wherever its name stands, `:ends get [first last];`.
            ::link~(::bind) ::link~(::any/get)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :message
              |~~ What the rule says of the literal, the template `get` builds it
                  from.
              ~~|
              ::string)
    | throws :BodyQuotedError
        |~~ Under a label the program a body writes bare is the verb,
            `:number/double mul 2;`, and a tilde writes code as a value, so
            a quote whose bare writing is that same program,
            `:number/double ~(mul 2);`, is refused, `:name` naming the
            declaration and `:found` the quote. A quote whose head asks for
            no subject is the verb it answers, `:Box/area ~(1);`.
            ::link~(::bind) ::link~(::quote)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :name |~~ The name declared. ~~| ::keyword
          | wires :found |~~ The quote written as the body. ~~| ::quote)
    | throws :NounNotDeclaredError
        |~~ A name with a path is declared under the noun its path names
            before its last name, and that noun is declared before it, so
            whether the noun is a kind, which the verbs under it serve, is
            known where they are declared: `:name` holds the name and
            `:noun` the noun no declaration made. The noun is declared
            first, a kind by its chain or by its page alone, and the names
            under it after.
            ::link~(::bind)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :name |~~ The name declared. ~~| ::keyword
          | wires :noun |~~ The noun its path names, which no declaration names. ~~| ::tag)
    | throws :WrittenOutsideItsChainError
        |~~ A kind and a function hold what they are in the chains that
            build them: an invariant is written
            `| holds :NotPositiveError |~~ … ~~| ~(gt 0)`, the payload and
            the fields of a kind `| accepts …` and `| wires …`, and an input
            of a verb `| takes …`,
            its answer `| returns …` and its subject `| on …`. Declared by
            a name, an invariant holds of no value, and an input, an
            answer or a subject under a verb is read by no call, so the
            declaration is refused, `:name` naming it and `:noun` the
            label or the verb it is under. A page and a law are declared
            by a name under any of them.
            ::link~(::kind) ::link~(::fn)
            ::link~(::bind)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :name
              |~~ The name declared outside the chain that holds what it
                  declares.
              ~~|
              ::keyword
          | wires :noun
              |~~ The label or the verb the name is declared under. ~~|
              ::tag)
    | throws :NameDeclaredTwiceError
        |~~ A name is declared once in a scope: the declarations of a text
            share its scope, and a declaration of a name one of them
            declared before is refused, `:name` the name, the failure of
            the text. A group and a quote open scopes of their own, where a
            name of the scope around them is shadowed, so a second value
            takes another name or a group of its own, `(:rate 3; rate)`.
            The recipe of a verb runs in a scope its call opens with `args`
            and each input, so a declaration of the recipe declares none of
            them again: the recipe reads an input by its name, and a value
            of its own takes another. An address the root holds is declared
            once at all: a reader extends a noun of the root by a name of its
            own, `:vec/second get /1;`, and replaces none of its names,
            and a source of the root that declares a name the root holds is
            refused.
            ::link~(::bind)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :name |~~ The name declared again. ~~| ::keyword)
    | throws :SubjectNotGivenError
        |~~ A body that declares no program reads no subject: the
            declarations of a text run before its query, with no value in
            the pipe, so a body that hands that absent value on, the fail
            track alone, `:x (!| 0);`, is refused, `:name` naming the
            declaration, and so is a verb called where no value is in the
            pipe, `:name` naming it. A body whose head is a verb declares a
            program, run on the subject wherever its name stands,
            `:n count; [1 2 3] | n` answering `3`; a value of the subject is
            named for the code of one step by `with`,
            `[1 2 3] | with {:n count} (map (add n))` answering
            `[4 5 6]`, and a value is computed once from values,
            `:n ([1 2 3] | count);`.
            ::link~(::any/with) ::link~(::bind)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :name |~~ The verb called in the body, or the declaration whose body hands on the value it is not given. ~~| ::keyword)
    | throws :ChainAnswersNoWordError
        |~~ A chain, a tag name and the commands joined to it up to the
            `;`, builds a word: a kind, a function, a page or a law, and
            under a noun the recipe of a verb. A chain that answers any
            other value is refused, `:name` naming the declaration and
            `:found` what the chain answered: the query after a declaration
            begins after the `;` that ends it,
            `:Box |~~ A box. ~~|; ::Box[1] | tag`.
            ::link~(::bind)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :name |~~ The name declared. ~~| ::keyword
          | wires :found |~~ What the chain answered. ~~|)
    | throws :PrintDeclaredError
        |~~ The print of a value is the language's literal, whatever its
            kind, the text that reads back as the value, which a host shows
            for an answer and `print` answers, so a `print` declared under
            a label is refused, `:name` naming it. A view of a value for
            reading is a verb of its own name under the label,
            `:Box/label get /name;`, which a reader asks for, `legend`
            the view of the language that glosses each part.
            ::link~(::any/print) ::link~(::any/legend)
            ::link~(::bind)
        ~~|
        ~(wires :name |~~ The name of the print declared under a label. ~~| ::keyword)
    | proves :PrintDeclaredError/refusesAPrintOfALabel
        |~~ A print declared under a label is refused. ~~|
        ~(~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~|; :Box/print ~("box")) | answer !| get [tag name] | eq [::bind/PrintDeclaredError :Box/print])
    | proves :BodyQuotedError/refusesTheQuoteOfAProgramUnderALabel
        |~~ A quote whose bare writing is the program of a verb is refused
            under its label.
        ~~|
        ~(~(:number/twice ~(mul 2); 5 | twice) | answer !| get [tag name] | eq [::bind/BodyQuotedError :number/twice])
    | proves :SubjectNotGivenError/refusesABodyOnTheFailTrackAlone
        |~~ A body that runs on the fail track alone hands on no value and is
            refused under its declaration.
        ~~|
        ~(~(:x (!| 0);) | answer !| get [tag name] | eq [::bind/SubjectNotGivenError :x])
    | proves :SubjectNotGivenError/takesAValueOfTheSubjectByWith
        |~~ A value of the subject is named for the code of one step by
            `with`.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | with {:n count} (map (add n)) | eq [4 5 6])
    | proves :ChainAnswersNoWordError/refusesAChainOfAValue
        |~~ A chain that answers a value and no word is refused, naming what
            it answered.
        ~~|
        ~(~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| ::vec | tag) | answer !| get [tag found] | eq [::bind/ChainAnswersNoWordError ::tag])
    | proves :answersTheAddressesItDeclared
        |~~ A text of declarations alone answers the set of the addresses
            it declared.
        ~~|
        ~(~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~|; :Box/area mulOf /0 /1) | answer | eq #[::Box ::Box/area])
    | proves :holdsTheNameAndTheBody
        |~~ A declaration holds its name and its body. ~~|
        ~(~(:inc add 1;) | first | get [/name /body/name] | eq [:inc :add])
    | proves :PayloadNotSchemaError/refusesDocsThatAreNoDocs
        |~~ Docs that are no vector of docs are refused, `:field` naming
            them.
        ~~|
        ~(::bind{:name :inc :docs [1]} !| get [tag field] | eq [::bind/PayloadNotSchemaError :docs])
    | proves :PayloadNotSchemaError/refusesATagForAName
        |~~ A tag is no name a declaration binds. ~~|
        ~({:name ::Box :body 1} | tag ::bind
          !| get [tag field] | eq [::bind/PayloadNotSchemaError :name])
    | proves :PayloadNotSchemaError/buildsTheDeclarationOfABody
        |~~ A keyword and the step a quote holds build the declaration. ~~|
        ~(get {:name :inc :body (~(add 1) | first)} | tag ::bind
          | get [it] | tag ::quote | eq ~(:inc add 1;))
    | proves :PayloadNotSchemaError/refusesABindOfNothing
        |~~ A bind with neither a body nor docs is refused under the body.
        ~~|
        ~(::bind{:name :inc} !| get [tag field] | eq [::bind/PayloadNotSchemaError :body])
    | proves :PayloadNotSchemaError/refusesABodyThatIsADoc
        |~~ A doc as the body is refused; it reads as the page of the
            declaration.
        ~~|
        ~(get {:name :note :body (~(|~~ Call back on Monday. ~~|) | first)} | tag ::bind
          !| get [tag field] | eq [::bind/PayloadNotSchemaError :body])
    | proves :PayloadNotSchemaError/bindsADocInAGroup
        |~~ A doc in a group is the value a declaration binds. ~~|
        ~(apply (:note (|~~ Call back on Monday. ~~|); note | content) | eq "Call back on Monday.")
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector is no record of a declaration. ~~|
        ~(::bind[1] !| get [tag expected] | eq [::bind/PayloadKindNotTakenError #[::map]])
    | proves :NameNotCallableError/refusesANameOfTwoWords
        |~~ A name with a space is no name a step calls. ~~|
        ~({:name :"a b" :body 1} | tag ::bind !| get [tag name] | eq [::bind/NameNotCallableError :"a b"])
    | proves :NameNotCallableError/refusesTheWordOfALiteral
        |~~ The word of a literal declares no value. ~~|
        ~({:name :true :body 1} | tag ::bind !| tag | eq ::bind/NameNotCallableError)
    | proves :LiteralComputesError/refusesANameInsideTheBody
        |~~ A body that is a vector of a name computes, and is refused. ~~|
        ~({:name :point :body [::call{:name :width}]} | tag ::bind !| tag | eq ::bind/LiteralComputesError)
    | proves :NounNotDeclaredError/refusesANameUnderNoNoun
        |~~ A name under a noun no declaration made is refused, naming the
            noun.
        ~~|
        ~(~(:Box/area ~(1)) | answer !| get [tag noun] | eq [::bind/NounNotDeclaredError ::Box])
    | proves :NounNotDeclaredError/takesANameUnderADeclaredNoun
        |~~ A name under a noun declared before it runs on the noun's
            values.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~|; :Box/area ~(1); ::Box{} | area | eq 1)
    | proves :WrittenOutsideItsChainError/refusesAnInvariantDeclaredBare
        |~~ An invariant declared by a bare name holds of no value. ~~|
        ~(~(:Positive ::invariant~(gt 0)) | answer !| get [tag name] | eq [::bind/WrittenOutsideItsChainError :Positive])
    | proves :WrittenOutsideItsChainError/refusesAnInvariantUnderALabel
        |~~ An invariant declared by a name under a label is refused; the
            chain of the kind writes it.
        ~~|
        ~(~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~|; :Pos/NotPositiveError ::invariant~(gt 0)) | answer !| get [tag name noun]
          |  eq [::bind/WrittenOutsideItsChainError :Pos/NotPositiveError ::Pos])
    | proves :WrittenOutsideItsChainError/refusesAnInputUnderAVerb
        |~~ An input declared by a name under a verb is refused; the chain
            of the function takes it.
        ~~|
        ~(~(:number/plus add 1; :number/plus/by /0) | answer !| tag | eq ::bind/WrittenOutsideItsChainError)
    | proves :WrittenOutsideItsChainError/refusesTheAnswerOfAVerb
        |~~ The answer of a verb declared by a name is refused. ~~|
        ~(~(:number/half div 2; :number/half/answer ::number) | answer !| tag | eq ::bind/WrittenOutsideItsChainError)
    | proves :WrittenOutsideItsChainError/takesAPageAndALaw
        |~~ A page and a law are declared by a name under a kind. ~~|
        ~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~| (::kind | accepts |~~ The number. ~~| ::number);
          :Pos/note |~~ A note. ~~|;
          :Pos/holdsFive ::law~(::Pos#5 | payload | eq 5);
          ::Pos | runLaws | map /ok | eq [true])
    | proves :NameDeclaredTwiceError/refusesASecondDeclaration
        |~~ A name declared again in its scope is refused. ~~|
        ~(~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~|; :Box |~~ A crate. ~~|) | answer !| get [tag name] | eq [::bind/NameDeclaredTwiceError :Box])
    | proves :NameDeclaredTwiceError/letsAGroupDeclareAgain
        |~~ A group declares in a scope of its own, so it may name a name
            again.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~|; (:Box |~~ A crate. ~~|; ::Box | doc | content) | eq "A crate.")
    | proves :NameDeclaredTwiceError/letsAnElementDeclareAgain
        |~~ A declaration inside an element stays in it. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~|; get [(:Box |~~ A crate. ~~|; ::Box | doc | content) (::Box | doc | content)] | eq ["A crate." "A box."])
    | proves :NameDeclaredTwiceError/letsAModifierDeclareAgain
        |~~ A declaration inside a modifier stays in it. ~~|
        ~({:rate 2} | with [/rate] (5 | mul (:rate 3; rate) | mul rate) | eq 30)
    | proves :NameDeclaredTwiceError/letsAQuoteDeclareAgain
        |~~ A quote declares in a scope of its own. ~~|
        ~({:rate 2} | with [/rate] (apply (:rate 3; rate) | add rate) | eq 5)
    | proves :NameDeclaredTwiceError/refusesAnInputDeclaredInTheRecipe
        |~~ The recipe of a verb declares none of its inputs again. ~~|
        ~(:number/plus (::fn | takes :by |~~ How much. ~~| /0 | impl ~(:by 3; add by)); 1 | plus 2 !| get [tag name] | eq [::bind/NameDeclaredTwiceError :by])
    | proves :NameDeclaredTwiceError/refusesANameTheRootHolds
        |~~ A reader declares no name the root holds. ~~|
        ~(~(:vec/count ~(0)) | answer !| tag | eq ::bind/NameDeclaredTwiceError)
    | proves :NameDeclaredTwiceError/extendsANounOfTheRoot
        |~~ A reader extends a noun of the root by a name of its own. ~~|
        ~(:vec/second get /1; [1 2] | second | eq 2)
    | impl ::builtin{};

